-- title:
--   Lemma 3.7 — multiplicative Chernoff bounds for adapted $[0,1]$-valued sequences
-- statement:
--   Let $(\Omega,\mathcal F,\mu)$ be a probability space with a filtration $\mathcal F_0\subseteq\mathcal F_1\subseteq\cdots$, and let $x_0,\dots,x_{T-1}$ be random variables with values in $[0,1]$ such that $x_t$ is $\mathcal F_{t+1}$-measurable. Write $m_t=\mathbb E[x_t\mid\mathcal F_t]$. For all $\varepsilon>0$ and $\delta>0$,
--
--   1. with probability at least $1-\delta$,
--   $$
--   \sum_{t}x_t-(1+\varepsilon)\sum_t m_t\le\frac{(1+\varepsilon)\ln(1/\delta)}{\varepsilon};
--   $$
--   2. with probability at least $1-\delta$,
--   $$
--   (1-\varepsilon)\sum_t m_t-\sum_t x_t\le\frac{(1+\varepsilon)\ln(1/\delta)}{\varepsilon}.
--   $$
--
--   With the natural filtration ($\mathcal F_0$ trivial, $\mathcal F_{t+1}$ generated by $x_0,\dots,x_t$) this is the paper's statement with $\mathbb E_{t-1}[x_t]=\mathbb E[x_t\mid x_1,\dots,x_{t-1}]$. It is the concentration tool used three times in the proof of Theorem 3.6.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Each clause is stated as: the event where the inequality fails has probability at most $\delta$. The conditional expectation is Mathlib's (an almost-everywhere defined version), which changes the events by null sets only.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: E_{t−1}[x_t] = E[x_t | x_1, …, x_{t−1}] → m_t = E[x_t | ℱ_t] for any filtration ℱ with x_t ℱ_{t+1}-measurable; reason: the natural filtration (ℱ_0 trivial, ℱ_{t+1} = σ(x_0, …, x_t)) gives the printed statement
--   - encoding: x_1, …, x_T → x : ℕ → Ω → ℝ, rounds 0, …, T − 1, range and measurability assumed for every index; reason: only the first T terms enter the sums; any finite sequence extends by 0
--   - encoding: with probability at least 1 − δ, inequality → μ(inequality fails) ≤ δ; reason: the same event statement
-- source:
--   Lykouris–Sridharan–Tardos, Small-loss bounds for online learning with partial information, arXiv:1711.03639v5, Lemma 3.7, p.12 (proof in Appendix A.1, pp.38–39)

import Mathlib

open scoped BigOperators
open MeasureTheory

namespace SmallLossGraph

/-- **Lemma 3.7 (multiplicative Chernoff bounds for adapted sequences).**
Lykouris–Sridharan–Tardos, arXiv:1711.03639v5, Lemma 3.7, p.12 (proof in Appendix A.1,
pp.38–39): "Let `x_1, x_2, …, x_T` be a sequence of non-negative random variables, s.t.
`x_t ∈ [0, 1]`. Let `E_{t−1}[x_t] = E[x_t | x_1, …, x_{t−1}]`. Then, for any `ε, δ > 0`, with
probability at least `1 − δ`
`∑_{t=1}^T x_t − (1 + ε) ∑_{t=1}^T E_{t−1}[x_t] ≤ (1 + ε) ln(1/δ)/ε`
and also with probability at least `1 − δ`
`(1 − ε) ∑_{t=1}^T E_{t−1}[x_t] − ∑_{t=1}^T x_t ≤ (1 + ε) ln(1/δ)/ε`."

Let `(Ω, μ)` be a probability space with a filtration `ℱ_0 ≤ ℱ_1 ≤ …`, and let `x_0, …, x_{T−1}`
be `[0,1]`-valued with `x_t` `ℱ_{t+1}`-measurable. Write `m_t = E[x_t | ℱ_t]`. For all `ε, δ > 0`,
each of the events
`∑_t x_t − (1+ε) ∑_t m_t > (1+ε) ln(1/δ)/ε` and `(1−ε) ∑_t m_t − ∑_t x_t > (1+ε) ln(1/δ)/ε`
has probability at most `δ`.

**Formalization Note** Rounds are 0-based: `x t` is the paper's `x_{t+1}` and `ℱ t` is the
information before it. The natural filtration (`ℱ_0` trivial, `ℱ_{t+1} = σ(x_0, …, x_t)`) gives
the printed statement, where `E[· | ℱ_0]` is the plain expectation. `μ[x t | ℱ t]` is Mathlib's
conditional expectation (an a.e.-defined version; the events change by null sets only). `ln` is
`Real.log`. -/
theorem lemma_3_7 {Ω : Type*} {m0 : MeasurableSpace Ω} (μ : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]
    (ℱ : Filtration ℕ m0) (T : ℕ) (x : ℕ → Ω → ℝ)
    (hx_meas : ∀ t, Measurable[ℱ (t + 1)] (x t))
    (hx_range : ∀ t ω, x t ω ∈ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1) (ε δ : ℝ) (hε : 0 < ε) (hδ : 0 < δ) :
    μ {ω | (1 + ε) * Real.log (1 / δ) / ε <
        ∑ t ∈ Finset.range T, x t ω - (1 + ε) * ∑ t ∈ Finset.range T, (μ[x t | ℱ t]) ω}
      ≤ ENNReal.ofReal δ ∧
    μ {ω | (1 + ε) * Real.log (1 / δ) / ε <
        (1 - ε) * ∑ t ∈ Finset.range T, (μ[x t | ℱ t]) ω - ∑ t ∈ Finset.range T, x t ω}
      ≤ ENNReal.ofReal δ := by sorry

end SmallLossGraph
